export const WEIGHTS_TEXT = "{\n  \"versionId\": \"2026.08.4\",\n  \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-02\",\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"source\": \"Irodora editorial curation — recommendation weights, 2026\",\n    \"sourceId\": \"IRO-ED-003\",\n    \"sourceType\": \"editorial\",\n    \"publisher\": null,\n    \"publishedYear\": null,\n    \"rightsHolder\": \"Irodora\",\n    \"sourceLicence\": \"Proprietary — Irodora original work\",\n    \"sourceUrl\": null,\n    \"derivation\": \"2026.08.4 SUPERSEDES 2026.08.3 AND CHANGES NOTHING THAT WAS IN IT: every existing occasion, every factor, weight and rationale, the falloff, the poles, the whole outfit block and the whole weather block are byte-identical, and a test compares the two files to assert that rather than trusting this sentence. WHAT IS ADDED IS FIVE OCCASION PROFILES — date, interview, travel, street and minimal — which FR-34 has named since it was written and which have never existed. The engine listed five occasions, the content published five, and the requirement named nine; nothing reported the gap because the two halves agreed with each other. NO WEIGHT HERE IS SUPPORTED BY A STUDY, and the rationales say what the reasoning was rather than what was observed — the standard the rest of this file sets, and a rationale that read like a measurement would be worse than none. EACH PROFILE IS A RE-ALLOCATION OF THE SAME FOUR NUMBERS and had to be defensible as a different argument rather than as a different mood, which is why the five are spread deliberately: interview is led by lightness and contrast because being unremarkable is its goal; minimal is nearly all lightness and contrast because removing chroma and temperature from the question is its definition; street is the only profile where chroma leads, and it reaches the same chroma weight as japanese-inspired from the opposite direction — restraint there, deliberate saturation here; travel is the one profile whose argument is about LIGHT rather than about dress, since warm-cool shifts most under the mixed illuminants of a long day; and date sits closest to default because it is the context least governed by convention. WHAT THIS FILE STILL CANNOT SAY is which occasion anybody is dressing for. Nothing selects one on any screen yet, so five more profiles reach no reader today — recorded here rather than left to be discovered, because content that reaches nothing is a shape this repository has learned to name.\",\n    \"authoredBy\": \"ed-001\",\n    \"authoredAt\": \"2026-09-02\",\n    \"verifiedBy\": \"ed-001\",\n    \"verifiedAt\": \"2026-09-02\",\n    \"reviewIndependence\": \"self\",\n    \"editorialNotes\": \"Reviewed by its own author (ADR-0060, OQ-5). What a second editor would most likely argue with: `street` gives chroma 0.35 and lightness 0.2, which makes it the profile most willing to accept a colour outside somebody’s personal lightness range — and a case can be made that a saturated colour is exactly when lightness matters most, because it is worn large. The counter-argument, and the reason it is written as it is, is that street dress has no convention about lightness at all, so a personal range is a fact about the person rather than about whether the outfit works in that context. The disagreement is real and this note exists so it can be had. Second most arguable: `travel` reduces temperature for a reason about ILLUMINANTS rather than about clothes, which is a different kind of argument from every other weight in this file.\"\n  },\n  \"falloff\": {\n    \"lightness\": 0.25,\n    \"chroma\": 0.12\n  },\n  \"poles\": {\n    \"warm\": 60,\n    \"cool\": 240\n  },\n  \"occasions\": [\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"default\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest of the four because it is the dimension a profile establishes most directly: three forced choices on one axis, each isolating hue with lightness and chroma held constant. It is also the factor a person can least easily judge for themselves, which is what makes it worth stating.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Close behind temperature. A lightness range is derived from the swatches somebody chose rather than asked about directly, so it is one inference further from the person — but it constrains the large areas of an outfit, which is where a mismatch is most visible.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"Lower because tolerance for chroma varies most within a single person: the same wearer accepts far more of it in a scarf than in a coat. Weighting it as heavily as lightness would make the engine refuse colours that work in small areas.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.15,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lightest weight, because it is the factor established with the least direct evidence — the guided flow infers it from three paired comparisons, and the photo path cannot establish it at all and abstains. A weight is not the place to compensate for that; the per-dimension confidence already does.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"office\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced from default. Office dress leans on a narrow, mostly neutral range where temperature differences are small by construction, so weighting it heavily would separate colours that read as interchangeable in the room.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Held at default. Lightness is what distinguishes one neutral from another once chroma is low, so it carries more of the decision here than it does elsewhere even at the same number.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.15,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced, and deliberately not to zero. Office palettes are low-chroma, so the axis discriminates less — but a strong colour is still a real choice a person can make, and a zero weight would stop the engine having any opinion about it.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Doubled from default, and the largest single move in this file. When chroma and temperature are both compressed, contrast is most of what remains to distinguish one office outfit from another.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"casual\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.4,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised. Casual dress has the widest colour range of any context here, so temperature is doing the most work separating what suits a person from what merely fits the occasion.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Slightly reduced. Casual outfits tolerate a wider lightness spread than office or formal dress, so a colour outside the range is a weaker signal than it is elsewhere.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised from default. This is the context where a saturated colour is most likely to be worn as a large area rather than an accent, which is exactly when chroma tolerance stops being a detail.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.1,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest weight in the file. Casual dress has no convention about separation between pieces, so a preference expressed here says more about the person than about whether the outfit works.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"formal\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest temperature weight here. Formal palettes are narrow and dark, and within that range the warm-cool distinction is small enough that weighting it heavily would produce confident answers about differences nobody sees.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest factor for this occasion. Formal dress is largely a question of how dark, and a colour outside the range is the most visible way to be wrong in it.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.1,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest chroma weight in the file. Formal palettes are near-neutral by convention, so the axis has very little range to discriminate over and a high weight would amplify noise.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"Tied for heaviest, for the same reason as the office profile and more so: when both chroma and temperature are compressed, the separation between pieces is most of what is left to judge.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"japanese-inspired\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Slightly below default. This corpus is roughly balanced between warm earths and cool indigos, so temperature separates its entries well — but the context is defined by restraint rather than by warmth, which is what the chroma weight expresses.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced. The traditional pairings this context draws on span a wide lightness range — 藍 against 生成り is most of the axis — so a narrow personal range is a weaker constraint here than elsewhere.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest chroma weight in the file, and the one that defines this profile. The restraint that characterises these palettes is a chroma property before it is anything else, so a colour outside the tolerance is the clearest way to be wrong in this context.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.1,\n          \"rationale\": \"Low. These pairings use both high contrast and near-tonal combinations freely, so a stated preference is a fact about the person rather than about the context, and the engine should weight it accordingly.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"date\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Slightly below default. Warmth still separates what suits somebody from what merely fits the room, but this is the one context judged by a single person at close range, and the axis that matters most there is how the colour sits against a face rather than which half of the wheel it came from.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced. Evening and restaurant light compresses the lightness range everybody is seen in, so a colour outside the personal range is a weaker signal here than it is under daylight — and the range was derived under daylight.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised from default and level with lightness. This is a context where a deliberate colour is read as a choice rather than as a mistake, so tolerance for chroma is doing real work and a low weight would flatten the profile toward the office.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised. Separation between pieces is most of how an outfit reads as considered at conversational distance, where the whole of it is in one view rather than glimpsed in passing.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"interview\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest temperature weight outside formal, and for the same reason: interview dress converges on a narrow, mostly neutral range where warm and cool differences are small by construction, so weighting the axis heavily would separate colours that read as interchangeable across a table.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest factor here. Being unremarkable is the goal of this profile, and how dark or light the largest pieces are is the most visible way to fail at it.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.1,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest weight in this profile, and deliberately not zero. A single strong colour is a real and defensible choice in an interview; the weight says the engine should have very little opinion about it rather than none.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"Tied for heaviest, on the office profile’s argument taken further. When temperature and chroma are both compressed, the separation between pieces is nearly all that remains to distinguish one interview outfit from another.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"travel\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced from default, and this is the profile where the reason is about LIGHT rather than about dress. Travel means many hours across daylight, aircraft cabin, terminal fluorescent and evening — and warm-cool is the axis that shifts most under mixed illuminants, so a confident answer about it is worth less here than anywhere else.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Held at default. Lightness is the most stable of the four under changing illuminants, which is exactly why it keeps its weight while temperature gives some up.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"Held at default. Nothing about travelling makes a person’s tolerance for saturation move; what changes is how reliably it can be judged, and that is temperature’s problem rather than this one’s.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised. Travel clothes are worn as layers added and removed through a day, so the separation between pieces is seen in more combinations than it is in any other context here.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"street\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.25,\n          \"rationale\": \"Reduced. This is the only profile in the file where chroma leads, and temperature is what gives way to it: a deliberate colour is the statement, and which side of the wheel it sits on is subordinate to how much of it there is.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"The lowest lightness weight in the file. Street dress uses the full range freely and the convention is that it may, so a colour outside a personal range says less about whether the outfit works than it does in any other context.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.35,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest chroma weight in the file, tied with japanese-inspired and arrived at from the opposite direction: that profile weights chroma because restraint is the point, and this one weights it because a saturated colour worn large is.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"Raised from default. Separation between pieces is part of how a deliberately assembled outfit reads as assembled rather than accidental, and this is the context most likely to use it hard.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"occasion\": \"minimal\",\n      \"factors\": [\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"temperature\",\n          \"weight\": 0.2,\n          \"rationale\": \"Low, and for the reason formal is: a near-neutral palette has very little warm-cool range to discriminate over, so a heavy weight would produce confident answers about differences nobody sees.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"lightness\",\n          \"weight\": 0.4,\n          \"rationale\": \"The heaviest single weight in this file. A minimal palette is defined by how light or dark each piece is and by almost nothing else — remove chroma and temperature from the question and this is what is left of it.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"chroma\",\n          \"weight\": 0.1,\n          \"rationale\": \"Tied for the lowest chroma weight in the file. Near-neutral is the definition of this context rather than a tendency within it, so the axis has the least range here of anywhere.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"factor\": \"contrast\",\n          \"weight\": 0.3,\n          \"rationale\": \"Doubled from default. When every piece is a neutral, the separation between them is the outfit — there is no other axis carrying the difference between two minimal outfits.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outfit\": {\n    \"harmony\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.2,\n      \"rationale\": \"Second heaviest. Whether the colours of an outfit relate to each other is the question an onlooker answers first, and it is the one an unaided eye is worst at predicting from the pieces separately.\"\n    },\n    \"personalFit\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.3,\n      \"rationale\": \"The heaviest of the six, and the only component resting on something the person told us directly — twelve forced choices, or a reading they confirmed. Every other component is a property of the clothes.\"\n    },\n    \"contrast\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.15,\n      \"rationale\": \"Middling. A contrast preference is real but coarse: three values, established with the least direct evidence of the four colour dimensions, and the photo path cannot establish it at all.\"\n    },\n    \"corpusAffinity\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.15,\n      \"rationale\": \"Deliberately equal to contrast rather than above it. This measures closeness to a curated corpus of Japanese colours — a real distance in dE00 — and NOT whether an outfit is beautiful or authentic. A heavier weight would let a proximity measure dominate an aesthetic question it cannot answer.\"\n    },\n    \"versatility\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.1,\n      \"rationale\": \"Low, because it answers a question about a wardrobe rather than about this outfit. A one-off combination that works is not worse for being hard to repeat, and weighting it heavily would push every recommendation toward neutrals.\"\n    },\n    \"cvdAccessibility\": {\n      \"weight\": 0.1,\n      \"rationale\": \"Low as a WEIGHT and not low in importance — this is the one component computed from a published model rather than from a convention, and F-032 is where a failing separation becomes a flag and an alternative rather than ten points off a score. Burying it in an overall is exactly what golden rule 13 warns against.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"weather\": {\n    \"mild\": {\n      \"harmony\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.2,\n        \"rationale\": \"The base outfit weighting, unchanged. Mild is the state in which no weather argument applies, and publishing it identical to the base block is what makes the other three legible as departures from something rather than as four unrelated sets.\"\n      },\n      \"personalFit\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.3,\n        \"rationale\": \"Unchanged from the base block. Personal fit is the only component resting on something the person told us directly, and nothing about mild weather makes that matter more or less.\"\n      },\n      \"contrast\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Whatever the weather, the differences between the pieces read the same way once somebody is in a room.\"\n      },\n      \"corpusAffinity\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Proximity to the curated corpus is a claim about the colours and not about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"versatility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Unchanged. Whether a combination repeats across a wardrobe is a question about the wardrobe rather than about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"cvdAccessibility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight, and deliberately never reduced in any weather. Separation for colour vision is an accessibility floor rather than a preference, and a weather profile that lowered it would be this product weighting a person out of its own answer.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"hot\": {\n      \"harmony\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.18,\n        \"rationale\": \"Slightly reduced. Hot weather narrows what is worn to fewer, lighter pieces, so there are fewer relationships for harmony to be about — not because harmony matters less, but because there is less of it in play.\"\n      },\n      \"personalFit\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.32,\n        \"rationale\": \"Raised, and it takes exactly what harmony gave up. With fewer garments each one is a larger share of what anybody sees, so how well a colour suits the person carries more of the outcome than it does under a coat.\"\n      },\n      \"contrast\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Whatever the weather, the differences between the pieces read the same way once somebody is in a room.\"\n      },\n      \"corpusAffinity\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Proximity to the curated corpus is a claim about the colours and not about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"versatility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held. A question about the wardrobe rather than about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"cvdAccessibility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight, and deliberately never reduced in any weather. Separation for colour vision is an accessibility floor rather than a preference, and a weather profile that lowered it would be this product weighting a person out of its own answer.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"cold\": {\n      \"harmony\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.25,\n        \"rationale\": \"Raised. Cold means layers, and layers mean more pieces visible at once — more pairs of colours in the same view, which is precisely what the harmony component measures.\"\n      },\n      \"personalFit\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.25,\n        \"rationale\": \"Reduced, and it gives exactly what harmony takes. Under layers a smaller area of any single colour sits near the face, so the personal question carries less of the outcome — it is still the component resting on what the person told us, which is why it stops level with harmony rather than below it.\"\n      },\n      \"contrast\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Whatever the weather, the differences between the pieces read the same way once somebody is in a room.\"\n      },\n      \"corpusAffinity\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Proximity to the curated corpus is a claim about the colours and not about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"versatility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held. A question about the wardrobe rather than about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"cvdAccessibility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight, and deliberately never reduced in any weather. Separation for colour vision is an accessibility floor rather than a preference, and a weather profile that lowered it would be this product weighting a person out of its own answer.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"wet\": {\n      \"harmony\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.175,\n        \"rationale\": \"Reduced. A wet day is the one where what somebody actually reaches for is decided by which pieces they are willing to get wet, so a ranking that leaned harder on harmony would be ranking outfits nobody is going to wear.\"\n      },\n      \"personalFit\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.275,\n        \"rationale\": \"Reduced by the same amount as harmony. This is the profile that concedes most to practicality, and the concession is spread across the two aesthetic components rather than taken out of one.\"\n      },\n      \"contrast\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Whatever the weather, the differences between the pieces read the same way once somebody is in a room.\"\n      },\n      \"corpusAffinity\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight. Proximity to the curated corpus is a claim about the colours and not about the day.\"\n      },\n      \"versatility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.15,\n        \"rationale\": \"Raised by half, and it is the only weight this file moves upward anywhere. Rain is when a combination that works across several other outfits earns its place, because it is the day somebody wears whatever is already reachable and dry. This is a preference and not an observation.\"\n      },\n      \"cvdAccessibility\": {\n        \"weight\": 0.1,\n        \"rationale\": \"Held at the base weight, and deliberately never reduced in any weather. Separation for colour vision is an accessibility floor rather than a preference, and a weather profile that lowered it would be this product weighting a person out of its own answer.\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n";
